---
/**
 * The privacy and security claims, in the words SECURITY.md is being reworded to.
 *
 * Every item is something the app's code or its CI can be held to: the update
 * check is the single named exception to the no-network claim, the supervisor's
 * MCP endpoint is the one socket Armada listens on, and
 * `scripts/audit-network.sh` (`make audit`) is what refuses a build that breaks either.
 * If that script is ever relaxed, this section changes in the same commit.
 *
 * The footnote is not optional. The `claude` Armada asks for plan limits does use
 * the network, on the user's own sign-in, and a privacy section that left it out
 * would be the most misleading thing on the page.
 */
import { DOWNLOAD } from "../config";
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";

const CLAIMS = [
  {
    title: "No network of its own",
    body: `Armada makes no network connection by itself. The one exception is the update check, off until you turn it on. It reads one file, <code>${DOWNLOAD.appcast}</code>, and sends no identifier: not your licence key, not a machine id. The one socket it listens on is the supervisor's MCP endpoint, also off until you turn it on, and on 127.0.0.1 only.`,
  },
  {
    title: "A licence checked on your Mac",
    body: "The key is verified offline, with a signature check. The app never contacts a licence server.",
  },
  {
    title: "Read only, where your agents write",
    body: "<code>~/.claude*</code> and <code>~/.codex</code> are opened read only. Armada never writes to Claude Code's or Codex's configuration, holds no vendor credentials, and never opens Codex's <code>auth.json</code>.",
  },
  {
    title: "Only its own files",
    body: "Its preferences, usage history in <code>~/Library/Application Support/io.mgcrea.armada</code> (kept 30 days, deletable from Settings), and a startup script in its own temporary folder when you start a session, with the connection details beside it when that session is a supervisor.",
  },
  {
    title: "Checked on every build",
    body: "<code>make audit</code> sweeps every Mach-O in the built app for URL loading, DNS and TLS symbols. Two allowances are narrowly named: Sparkle, the update framework, and the MCP endpoint, which must bind 127.0.0.1 and nothing else.",
  },
  {
    title: "Out of your way",
    body: "A menu bar app with no Dock icon. A session you start or fork runs in your own terminal, and Armada never owns the agent process.",
  },
] as const;

const code = "[&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.9em] [&_code]:break-words [&_code]:text-fg";
---

<section id="private" class="wrap scroll-mt-20 py-[96px]">
  <SectionHeader eyebrow="Privacy" heading="Stays out of your way.<br />Private by construction.">
    Armada reads every agent transcript on your Mac, so the claim worth making is that none of it
    leaves. Here is what it does and does not do, and what checks it.
  </SectionHeader>

  <dl class:list={["m-0 grid grid-cols-1 gap-x-10 gap-y-0 md:grid-cols-2", code]}>
    {
      CLAIMS.map((c) => (
        <div class="reveal flex flex-col gap-2 border-t border-rule py-6">
          <dt class="text-[15.5px] font-medium text-fg">{c.title}</dt>
          <dd class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.65] text-pretty text-fg-dim" set:html={c.body} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <p
    class:list={[
      "mt-6 mb-0 max-w-[68ch] text-[13.5px] leading-[1.65] text-pretty text-fg-muted",
      code,
    ]}
  >
    Armada is not sandboxed, by design: it has to read other tools' folders. And one program it runs
    does use the network. The <code>claude</code> it asks for your plan limits talks to Anthropic
    over your own sign-in, as it does whenever you use it. The full inventory is on the
    <a href="/privacy" class="text-fg underline decoration-rule-2 underline-offset-4">privacy page</a>.
  </p>
</section>
